many of these migrants have been waiting  in tijuana mexico for years hoping to  seek asylum in the u.s  but along with thousands of others  they'll have to wait longer  a federal judge has extended an  immigration policy known as title 42  which blocks most of them from claiming  asylum  we've been waiting for so long for years  with title 42 in place we're not allowed  to request asylum god willing we can  find a solution because returning to our  countries isn't really an option  the policy introduced under donald  trump's administration allows the  government to expel migrants from the  u.s to prevent the spread of covet 19.  president joe biden was due to lift the  measure on monday but a coalition of 24  states sued and won  we understand title 42 exists due to  sanitary measures that's why we're here  today with proof of vaccination and our  cova tests we don't have the virus so i  don't think it's fair to apply this  order against us  more than 230 000 migrants crossed the  border between mexico and the u.s in  april alone without title 42 u.s  officials say the numbers could surge to  18 000 migrants a day  the city is at full capacity right now  the shelters are overcrowded and there  are many people who've been waiting for  a long time there's a significant  haitian population families and many  pregnant women with families there's a  lot of uncertainty and a lot of  frustration  it's a problem for biden whose approval  ratings are low republicans are also  focusing on the issue ahead of the  midterm elections in november  human rights groups say the crisis is  being politicized by both sides  the justice department says it will  appeal the decision in the meantime  thousands of migrants will be forced to  wait yet again  katya lopez  al jazeera
 

